197	<script>
198	const D = {"focal_event":{"id":"F0","statement":"SIF near miss, 14 Oct 2024: at approximately 13:03 the R3 delivery-side carrier beam (approx. 8,500 lb, with designed keeper still installed on the link arm) fell into the R3 mill pit and struck the sled and scaffold platform during outage work; contractor employees had worked in the pit earlier that day, none were in the pit below the beam at the time of the fall, and no injury in the pit was confirmed.","source_ids":["R3_Incident_Context_ONLY_Slides_1-2#slide2","R3-E05","R3-E09","R3-E04"]},"nodes":[{"id":"N01","statement":"The falling carrier beam impacted the sled and scaffold platform in the R3 mill pit, causing damage.","epistemic_status":"observed_event","cause_type":"transitory","source_ids":["R3_Incident_Context_ONLY_Slides_1-2#slide2","R3-E05","R3-E09"]},{"id":"N02","statement":"The sled and scaffold platform were located in the mill pit within the beam's fall path.","epistemic_status":"observed_event","cause_type":"non_transitory","source_ids":["R3_Incident_Context_ONLY_Slides_1-2#slide2","R3-E09"]},{"id":"N03","statement":"The falling beam delivered force greater than the sled/scaffold platform could withstand (fall height and impact energy not measured).","epistemic_status":"reported_interpretation","cause_type":"non_transitory","source_ids":["R3-E05","R3-E09","R3-E04","CAUSE_PATTERNS"]},{"id":"N04","statement":"At approximately 13:03 the delivery-side carrier beam fell into the R3 mill pit.","epistemic_status":"observed_event","cause_type":"transitory","source_ids":["R3-E05","R3_Incident_Context_ONLY_Slides_1-2#slide2"]},{"id":"N05","statement":"Immediately before the fall, the carrier beam was at a comparatively high elevation above the pit.","epistemic_status":"observed_event","cause_type":"non_transitory","source_ids":["R3-E05","R3-E01","R3-E02"]},{"id":"N06","statement":"The carrier-beam assembly has a mass of approximately 8,500 lb.","epistemic_status":"reported_interpretation","cause_type":"non_transitory","source_ids":["R3-E04"]},{"id":"N07","statement":"Fall initiated: at ~13:03 the beam changed from a supported/constrained state to free fall (the precise trigger and the instant of change were not directly observed).","epistemic_status":"unresolved_premise","cause_type":"transitory","source_ids":["R3-E05","R3-E06","R3-E09"]},{"id":"N08","statement":"No independently verified engineered mechanical prop, pin, cribbing or blocking device was supporting the raised carrier-beam/yoke system at the time of the fall (packet records neither its installation nor its absence).","epistemic_status":"unresolved_premise","cause_type":"non_transitory","source_ids":["R3-E01","R3-E02","R3-E09"]},{"id":"N09","statement":"The surface(s) carrying the beam's weight immediately before 13:03 (link-arm/yoke connection and/or contact with mill housing, spreader or post structure) ceased to carry it.","epistemic_status":"unresolved_premise","cause_type":"transitory","source_ids":["R3-E05","R3-E06","R3-E07"]},{"id":"N10A","statement":"Candidate release mode A: the link arm passed off the yoke because keeper/yoke overlap was lost under elevated, laterally offset or constrained geometry.","epistemic_status":"proposed_mechanism","cause_type":"transitory","source_ids":["R3-E04","R3-E05","R3-E06"]},{"id":"N10B","statement":"Candidate release mode B: the beam, which had been held up by contact with adjacent mill housing/spreader/post structure while the yoke descended, slipped or shifted off that contact.","epistemic_status":"proposed_mechanism","cause_type":"transitory","source_ids":["R3-E04","R3-E05"]},{"id":"N11","statement":"During the roughly two hours before the fall, relative vertical displacement developed between the descending yoke and the beam, which stayed comparatively high; any resulting change in link-arm/yoke engagement geometry was not measured.","epistemic_status":"reported_interpretation","cause_type":"transitory","source_ids":["R3-E05","R3-E04","R3-E07"]},{"id":"N12","statement":"The yoke moved downward gradually and progressively over roughly the two hours leading toward the fall (described by video reviewers; no instrumented travel or speed measurement).","epistemic_status":"observed_event","cause_type":"transitory","source_ids":["R3-E05"]},{"id":"N13","statement":"While the yoke's elevation changed, the beam appeared to be constrained near the surrounding mill housing, spreader or post structure.","epistemic_status":"reported_interpretation","cause_type":"non_transitory","source_ids":["R3-E05"]},{"id":"N14","statement":"With the backup rolls removed, the raised assembly occupied an upper region near the mill spreader and posts that it does not normally occupy during roll-present operation; first-contact time and remaining clearance were not established.","epistemic_status":"proposed_mechanism","cause_type":"non_transitory","source_ids":["R3-E01","R3-E04","R3-E02"]},{"id":"N15","statement":"The R3 backup-roll set had been removed on the preceding turn as part of planned fall-outage work.","epistemic_status":"observed_event","cause_type":"transitory","source_ids":["R3-E01","R3-E02"]},{"id":"N16","statement":"The delivery-side top roll-balance assembly (yoke, link arms, carrier beam) was held in an elevated position to give working clearance for the stripper-guide-box replacement.","epistemic_status":"observed_event","cause_type":"transitory","source_ids":["R3-E01","R3-E02"]},{"id":"N17","statement":"As designed, installed backup rolls limit upper travel of the lifting arrangement; no separate positive stop preventing the raised carrier beam from contacting adjacent upper housing structure is identified in the as-built excerpt.","epistemic_status":"reported_interpretation","cause_type":"non_transitory","source_ids":["R3-E04","R3-E08"]},{"id":"N18","statement":"Lateral offset of the link arm relative to the yoke at incident time (possible relative lateral displacement seen on video; magnitude not convertible to a clearance).","epistemic_status":"unresolved_premise","cause_type":"undetermined","source_ids":["R3-E05","R3-E06"]},{"id":"N19","statement":"The connection design uses a single keeper on each link arm; post-event, the original keeper hardware was still installed on the link arm.","epistemic_status":"observed_event","cause_type":"non_transitory","source_ids":["R3-E06","R3-E08","R3-E05","R3_Incident_Context_ONLY_Slides_1-2#slide2"]},{"id":"N20","statement":"Post-event inspection found measurable wear at the link-arm/yoke interfaces; in normal centered/resting geometry, wear plus nominal dimensions did not give enough clearance for the link arm to pass off the yoke.","epistemic_status":"observed_event","cause_type":"non_transitory","source_ids":["R3-E06"]},{"id":"N21","statement":"The hydraulic holding force in the roll-balance lifting cylinders decreased while the assembly was raised.","epistemic_status":"proposed_mechanism","cause_type":"transitory","source_ids":["R3-E07"]},{"id":"N22","statement":"During a separate scheduled hydraulic job, the supply designated 'B hydraulic system' was shut down and isolated while the stripper-guide-box changeout was underway and the roll-balance assembly was raised.","epistemic_status":"observed_event","cause_type":"transitory","source_ids":["R3-E03"]},{"id":"N23","statement":"The B-system supply and the roll-balance cylinder feed are hydraulically connected (same or connected oil path) in the incident configuration.","epistemic_status":"unresolved_premise","cause_type":"non_transitory","source_ids":["R3-E03","R3-E07"]},{"id":"N24","statement":"Slow internal leakage/bleed-through at an isolation valve let fluid redistribute or return along a connected line after the supply was shut down.","epistemic_status":"proposed_mechanism","cause_type":"non_transitory","source_ids":["R3-E07"]},{"id":"N25","statement":"The raised top roll-balance assembly was locally isolated at the valve stand (isolation planned/logged; holding effectiveness not verified by pressure trace).","epistemic_status":"reported_interpretation","cause_type":"non_transitory","source_ids":["R3-E02","R3-E03"]},{"id":"N26","statement":"For some hydraulic isolation valves, replacement was historically initiated after a problem was found rather than after a fixed, comprehensive leakage measurement at every outage.","epistemic_status":"reported_interpretation","cause_type":"non_transitory","source_ids":["R3-E08"]},{"id":"N27","statement":"The yoke descent continued for roughly two hours with no recorded intervention that arrested it or mechanically secured the beam; whether anyone observed the drift is not recorded.","epistemic_status":"unresolved_premise","cause_type":"undetermined","source_ids":["R3-E05","R3-E09","R3-E02"]},{"id":"N28","statement":"The task packet controlled roll-balance energy through local valve-stand isolation and a reference to the site lockout/tryout process; it does not record a verified mechanical block under the raised beam/yoke or a measured clearance to surrounding structures.","epistemic_status":"observed_event","cause_type":"non_transitory","source_ids":["R3-E02"]},{"id":"N29","statement":"The raised, roll-removed outage configuration differs from routine operation with the backup rolls in place (non-routine task geometry).","epistemic_status":"observed_event","cause_type":"non_transitory","source_ids":["R3-E01","R3-E04"]},{"id":"N30","statement":"Maintenance practice, as summarized, distinguishes inspecting serviceable components from proving safety-critical clearances in a roll-removed, fully elevated outage configuration; no such clearance proof is documented in the extract.","epistemic_status":"reported_interpretation","cause_type":"non_transitory","source_ids":["R3-E08"]},{"id":"N31","statement":"The effect of isolating the B hydraulic system on the holding of the raised roll-balance assembly was not identified before the isolation was carried out.","epistemic_status":"unresolved_premise","cause_type":"non_transitory","source_ids":["R3-E03"]},{"id":"N32","statement":"Job paperwork uses inconsistent naming: a maintenance form designates a system supply by a single letter, while an older equipment sketch uses a letter for a different component grouping; no signed valve-by-valve flow schematic for the incident configuration is in the packet.","epistemic_status":"observed_event","cause_type":"non_transitory","source_ids":["R3-E03"]},{"id":"N33","statement":"The separate hydraulic job was scheduled and taken up while the stripper-guide-box changeout was underway.","epistemic_status":"observed_event","cause_type":"transitory","source_ids":["R3-E03"]},{"id":"N34","statement":"Contractor employees worked in the R3 mill pit throughout the morning preparing for the stripper-guide-box work while the assembly was raised; none were in the pit directly below the beam at 13:03.","epistemic_status":"observed_event","cause_type":"transitory","source_ids":["R3_Incident_Context_ONLY_Slides_1-2#slide2","R3-E09"]},{"id":"N35","statement":"The stripper-guide-box job scope required personnel access around the R3 stand and in the mill pit.","epistemic_status":"observed_event","cause_type":"non_transitory","source_ids":["R3-E02","R3-E09"]}],"causal_connections":[{"effect_id":"F0","cause_ids":["N01","N34"],"combination":"AND","support_status":"supported","mechanism":"The SIF near-miss classification requires both the damaging beam impact and personnel exposure in the strike zone during the hazard window; take away either and the event is equipment damage only, or no event.","source_ids":["R3_Incident_Context_ONLY_Slides_1-2#slide2","R3-E09"]},{"effect_id":"N01","cause_ids":["N04","N02","N03"],"combination":"AND","support_status":"supported","mechanism":"Impact damage pattern: something moving (falling beam), something in its path (sled/scaffold), and force exceeding the struck item's capacity. All three were present and the damage was observed.","source_ids":["R3-E05","R3-E09","CAUSE_PATTERNS"]},{"effect_id":"N03","cause_ids":["N06","N05"],"combination":"AND","support_status":"supported","mechanism":"Impact energy scales with falling mass and drop height. A reported ~8,500 lb mass falling from an elevated position into the pit gives enough energy to damage a sled/scaffold; the exact height and energy were not measured.","source_ids":["R3-E04","R3-E05","CAUSE_PATTERNS"]},{"effect_id":"N04","cause_ids":["N07","N05","N06"],"combination":"AND","support_status":"supported","mechanism":"Object-fell-from-height pattern: the beam was at elevation, has mass, and under gravity falls once support is lost (fall initiated). The edge is physically necessary, but the content of N07 (the trigger) remains unresolved.","source_ids":["R3-E05","R3-E04","CAUSE_PATTERNS"]},{"effect_id":"N05","cause_ids":["N16","N13"],"combination":"AND","support_status":"proposed","mechanism":"The beam was raised for working clearance (N16) and, by the reviewers' description, stayed high while the yoke dropped (N13). Testable by reviewing video frames for beam elevation against yoke elevation over time.","source_ids":["R3-E01","R3-E02","R3-E05"]},{"effect_id":"N07","cause_ids":["N09","N08"],"combination":"AND","support_status":"unresolved","mechanism":"A raised load falls only if its active support fails and no independent mechanical support catches it. Whether a block or prop was present, and whether it was adequate, is not established.","source_ids":["R3-E02","R3-E05","R3-E09"]},{"effect_id":"N09","cause_ids":["N10A","N10B"],"combination":"UNSPECIFIED","support_status":"proposed","mechanism":"The beam's last support was lost through link-arm disengagement from the yoke, by slipping off the structural contact, or by a sequence of both (beam hung on structure, yoke overlap lost, then structural contact released). Testable by reconstructing incident geometry and examining contact/witness marks on the spreader, posts, link arms and yoke.","source_ids":["R3-E04","R3-E05","R3-E06","R3-E07"]},{"effect_id":"N10A","cause_ids":["N11","N18","N19","N20"],"combination":"UNSPECIFIED","support_status":"proposed","mechanism":"Single-keeper retention depends on overlap geometry. The post-event check shows centered geometry plus wear does not allow pass-off, so disengagement would also need vertical separation and/or lateral offset not present at rest. Testable by measuring keeper/yoke overlap under the reconstructed elevated, offset geometry. The keeper being found installed rules out a missing keeper, not disengagement around it.","source_ids":["R3-E04","R3-E05","R3-E06","R3-E08"]},{"effect_id":"N10B","cause_ids":["N13","N11"],"combination":"AND","support_status":"proposed","mechanism":"If structural contact was carrying the beam while the yoke dropped away beneath it, then once that contact released the link-arm/yoke path may no longer have been in a position to catch the load. Testable by locating contact marks and comparing beam and yoke positions on video just before 13:03.","source_ids":["R3-E05","R3-E07","R3-E04"]},{"effect_id":"N11","cause_ids":["N12","N13"],"combination":"AND","support_status":"proposed","mechanism":"Relative displacement develops only if the yoke moves while the beam is held from moving with it. The video describes both together; the constraint itself is an interpretation from an incomplete view.","source_ids":["R3-E05","R3-E07"]},{"effect_id":"N13","cause_ids":["N14","N16"],"combination":"AND","support_status":"proposed","mechanism":"With the assembly raised into the upper region near the spreader and posts, it can contact and be constrained by those structures. Testable by measuring the vertical and lateral clearance envelope in the roll-removed raised position.","source_ids":["R3-E04","R3-E05","R3-E01"]},{"effect_id":"N14","cause_ids":["N15","N16","N17"],"combination":"AND","support_status":"proposed","mechanism":"Backup rolls normally cap upper travel. With them removed, raising the assembly and having no separate positive stop allows it to reach structures it does not normally reach. The mechanism is stated in the engineering reference; incident-time contact is not measured.","source_ids":["R3-E01","R3-E04","R3-E08"]},{"effect_id":"N12","cause_ids":["N21","N27"],"combination":"AND","support_status":"unresolved","mechanism":"With holding force reduced, the yoke would lower under load. That it kept lowering for about two hours also requires that nothing arrested the drift or secured the beam. Neither the hydraulic cause nor the detection status is established.","source_ids":["R3-E05","R3-E07"]},{"effect_id":"N21","cause_ids":["N22","N23","N24","N25"],"combination":"AND","support_status":"unresolved","mechanism":"Candidate hydraulic chain: after the B supply was shut down, cylinder pressure could bleed away only if the cylinders connect to that path and the local isolation leaked internally. Testable with a signed valve-by-valve schematic, a leak-rate test of the suspected valve, and a pressure-decay test in the reconstructed configuration.","source_ids":["R3-E03","R3-E07"]},{"effect_id":"N24","cause_ids":["N26"],"combination":"UNSPECIFIED","support_status":"unresolved","mechanism":"A run-to-failure replacement practice with no periodic leakage measurement could let internal leakage go undetected. Whether this specific valve was leaking, and its history, is not in the packet.","source_ids":["R3-E08","R3-E07"]},{"effect_id":"N22","cause_ids":["N33","N16","N31"],"combination":"AND","support_status":"proposed","mechanism":"The isolation happened while the assembly was raised because a separate job was taken up during the changeout. Going ahead with it suggests its effect on the raised assembly was not identified. That last premise is unverified: the planner's reasoning and any hazard review are not recorded.","source_ids":["R3-E03","R3-E01"]},{"effect_id":"N31","cause_ids":["N32","N23"],"combination":"UNSPECIFIED","support_status":"unresolved","mechanism":"If the B-system and the roll-balance feed connect, inconsistent letter labeling and the lack of a signed flow schematic would make the connection hard to see from the paperwork. This explains why the isolation could have seemed reasonable at the time (cause+2), but no testimony or planning record confirms it.","source_ids":["R3-E03"]},{"effect_id":"N08","cause_ids":["N28","N29","N30"],"combination":"UNSPECIFIED","support_status":"unresolved","mechanism":"If no mechanical block was in place, a plausible explanation is that the task plan relied on valve-stand isolation and LOTO for a non-routine, roll-removed configuration whose clearance and holding hazards had not been proven. Testable by physical evidence of blocking, crew and planner interviews, and the task hazard assessment.","source_ids":["R3-E02","R3-E01","R3-E08"]},{"effect_id":"N34","cause_ids":["N35"],"combination":"UNSPECIFIED","support_status":"supported","mechanism":"The job scope required access in the pit, so contractor personnel were exposed beneath the raised assembly during the morning preparation work.","source_ids":["R3-E02","R3-E09","R3_Incident_Context_ONLY_Slides_1-2#slide2"]}],"unresolved_conditions":[{"question":"Was an engineered mechanical prop, pin, cribbing or blocking device installed under the raised carrier-beam/yoke system on the incident shift, and was it adequate for the roll-removed geometry?","related_node_ids":["N08","N28","N07"],"why_unresolved":"The task packet and handover give no dated, verified record either way. Missing documentation is not evidence that a control was absent, and no witness saw the underside."},{"question":"Do the 'B hydraulic system' supply and the roll-balance cylinder feed share or connect to the same oil path in the incident configuration?","related_node_ids":["N23","N31","N32","N21"],"why_unresolved":"Naming conventions differ across documents, and no signed valve-by-valve schematic for the incident configuration was supplied."},{"question":"Did the local isolation valve leak internally at incident time, at what rate, and was that enough to produce about two hours of yoke descent?","related_node_ids":["N24","N21","N12","N26"],"why_unresolved":"Bleed-through is only an engineering hypothesis. There is no leak-rate test, event-time pressure trace or valve examination."},{"question":"When was the B system shut down relative to the start of the yoke descent seen on video?","related_node_ids":["N22","N12"],"why_unresolved":"The dispatch extract records sequence but not timestamps that can be matched to the video. Without temporal correlation, the hydraulic branch cannot be confirmed."},{"question":"Are there other causes of yoke descent (e.g., other hydraulic leakage paths) that were not evaluated?","related_node_ids":["N21","N12"],"why_unresolved":"The packet evaluates only one candidate hydraulic mechanism and does not show whether alternatives were ruled out."},{"question":"What released the beam at 13:03: link-arm/yoke disengagement, release from structural contact, or a sequence of both?","related_node_ids":["N09","N10A","N10B","N07"],"why_unresolved":"The video does not show the full keeper/yoke contact surface or the instant of release. No qualified witness observed the transition, and post-event inspection cannot directly measure incident-time geometry."},{"question":"What were the lateral offset and the keeper/yoke overlap at incident time, and could the link arm pass off the yoke under that elevated, offset geometry?","related_node_ids":["N18","N19","N20","N10A"],"why_unresolved":"The post-event check covered only normal centered geometry. The lateral displacement seen on video cannot be converted to a clearance."},{"question":"When did the raised assembly first contact the spreader/post/housing structure, and how much vertical and lateral clearance remained?","related_node_ids":["N14","N13","N17"],"why_unresolved":"The handover and task packet contain no position or clearance measurement, and the video shows only apparent constraint."},{"question":"Did anyone observe the two-hour yoke descent, and what monitoring or hold-point requirements applied to a raised assembly during concurrent jobs?","related_node_ids":["N27","N12"],"why_unresolved":"Interviews cover work sequencing and location only. No record shows whether the drift was noticed or what monitoring was required."},{"question":"Why did relying on valve-stand isolation and concurrent scheduling of the B-system job make sense to the planners and crews at the time (procedure clarity, hazard assessment of the roll-removed configuration, training, schedule pressure)?","related_node_ids":["N28","N29","N30","N31","N33"],"why_unresolved":"There is no planner or crew testimony, task hazard assessment, or coordination record for the two jobs. Human-action branches cannot be taken past the documented actions without inventing reasoning."},{"question":"What were the drop height and impact energy, and what was the potential consequence had personnel been in the pit below the beam?","related_node_ids":["N03","N34","N05"],"why_unresolved":"No elevation measurement or impact analysis is provided. Potential impact can only be stated qualitatively."}],"investigation_conclusion":{"status":"partial","summary":"The proximate physical chain is supported: an ~8,500 lb carrier beam raised for stripper-guide-box clearance (backup rolls removed) lost support at about 13:03, fell into the pit and struck the sled/scaffold platform. Pit access for the job had exposed contractors earlier that day. The video supports a precursor: the yoke descended over about two hours while the beam, apparently constrained near the spreader/post structure, stayed high, causing relative displacement. Removing the backup rolls without a separate positive stop plausibly let the assembly reach that structure. The release trigger is not established. Link-arm disengagement around a still-installed single keeper and release from structural contact remain proposed alternatives; post-event inspection excludes simple pass-off in centered geometry but not in offset or elevated geometry. The cause of yoke descent is also not established. B-system isolation during a concurrent hydraulic job, a connected oil path and valve bleed-through form a candidate AND-chain, but the shared path and the leak are both unverified. Two jointly required conditions remain unresolved: whether independent mechanical blocking was absent, and whether the isolation's effect on the raised assembly went unrecognized (possibly because of inconsistent system labeling). The board does not support declaring a root cause. Candidate systemic contributors are reliance on hydraulic isolation for a non-routine raised configuration, no proven clearance and holding checks for roll-removed outages, reactive valve replacement, and concurrent-job energy coordination. Each needs the listed evidence before it can be confirmed."}};
